{
  "image_name": "d0a54c3a84a427ae800421f71f9b018a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares the layered structure of a conventional graphite-based battery cell with a metal-based cell, emphasizing how the anode changes the overall thickness and composition. In both schematics, a cathode made of packed particles sits beneath a top current collector and contains binder plus conductive additives. The graphite-based cell on the left has a relatively thick anode made of many graphite particles above a bottom current collector, with a separate electrolyte layer between cathode and anode. Its total stack thickness is much larger, extending close to 200 µm.\n\nBy contrast, the metal-based cell on the right replaces the bulky graphite anode with a thin metal layer, so the anode region is much thinner. The cathode still contains particulate material, but the label suggests binder and catholyte/conductive additives are integrated there. The electrolyte layer remains between electrodes, and the current collectors are retained on both sides. A vertical arrow labeled “Charge” appears beside the metal-based cell, indicating operation during charging. Overall, the visual message is that using a metal anode can significantly reduce inactive or low-energy-volume thickness and produce a more compact cell stack.",
